  Read quality control and adapter trimming were performed with fastp (v0.24.0) using the following parameters: minimum Phred quality score of 10, maximum 40% low-quality bases per read, maximum 5 ambiguous bases, minimum read length of 36 bp after trimming, 3′ sliding-window quality trimming (window size 4, mean quality 20), and poly-G/poly-X tail trimming. Trimmed reads were aligned to the human reference genome (GRCh38, GENCODE v49 primary assembly) using STAR (v2.7.11b). Gene-level read counts were quantified with featureCounts (Subread package, v2.0.3) using GENCODE v49 annotations on uniquely mapped reads. Differential gene expression analysis was performed using edgeR (v4.8.2). Raw counts were filtered to remove lowly expressed genes using the filterByExpr function with default parameters. Library sizes were normalized using the trimmed mean of M-values (TMM) method. A negative binomial generalized linear model was fitted with quasi-likelihood estimation (glmQLFit) to account for overdispersion. For paired experimental designs, sample pairing was incorporated into the design matrix as a blocking factor (~0 + pair + group). Comparisons were performed using contrast with glmQLFTest between Non-target and TANK KO groups. FDR was adjusted p values using the Benjamini-Hochberg method.

  B-cell focused gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA) was performed using fgsea (v1.36.2) with the fgseaMultilevel function. All expressed genes were ranked by log2 fold change from the differential expression analysis. GSEA was conducted against collections retrieved from MSigDB via msigdbr: a B-cell relevant subset of C2 filtered by pathway names associated with B-cell biology ("B_CELL_RECEPTOR", "BCR", "B_CELL", "NF_KAPPA_B_IN_B_CELLS", "SIGNALING_BY_THE_B_CELL_RECEPTOR", "ANTIGEN_ACTIVATES_B_CELL", "B_LYMPHOCYTE", "IMMUNOGLOBULIN", "PLASMA_CELL", "CLASS_SWITCH", "SOMATIC_HYPERMUTATION", "MHC_CLASS_II", "B_CELL_ACTIVATION"), gene sets with fewer than 15 or more than 500 genes were excluded. Statistical significance was assessed using 10,000 permutations and p-values were adjusted using the Benjamini-Hochberg method; gene sets with FDR-adjusted p-value < 0.05 were considered significant.
